\begin{abstract}
For a composite modulus $N$ and base $b = \lceil\sqrt N\,\rceil$, the \emph{sieve polynomial} is $v(j) = (b+j)^2 - N$, and a \emph{hit} at position $j$ is a position whose value $v(j)$ is $B$-smooth. Extensive sampling on balanced $96$-bit semiprimes shows that hits cluster toward small $j$: pooled over $9565$ hits from $127$ moduli, the rescaled hit position deviates from uniformity with Kolmogorov--Smirnov statistic $D = 0.09519$ against a paired null control at $D = 0.00693$, with a monotone declining decile profile from $0.162$ down to $0.072$. This paper develops the exact arithmetic theory needed to interpret such a signal, separating the \emph{magnitude} channel from the \emph{positional} channel.

We prove: (i) the \textbf{Position--GCD Law} $\gcd(j, v(j)) = \gcd(j, v(0))$, so the entire arithmetic interaction between a position and its own value is fixed by the single integer $v(0) = b^2 - N$; (ii) a \textbf{free cofactor reduction} turning this into a genuine, magnitude-free smoothness enrichment; (iii) that this \emph{gcd carrier} is nevertheless \textbf{positionally uniform}, being periodic of period $|v(0)|$, as is divisibility by any fixed prime (at most two residue classes) or prime power; (iv) a general \textbf{local discrepancy bound}: a residue-determined position property has window counts differing by at most its modulus, so an observed block excess of $E$ hits forces every local explanation to have modulus $T \ge E$; (v) that the unique declining local carrier is full self-divisibility $j \mid v(j) \iff j \mid v(0)$, with exact density $1/j$ and a proved \textbf{harmonic block decline} yielding a strict small-$j$ excess; (vi) the \textbf{magnitude sandwich} $2bj \le v(j) \le 2bj + j^2 + 2b$ and the resulting \textbf{cell collapse}, showing that stratifying by $|v|$ within one $N$ confines positions to a factor-two window and therefore cannot decorrelate position from magnitude; (vii) \textbf{non-locality of the smooth locus}: no modulus and no set of residues describes the positions carrying smooth values; and (viii) exact \textbf{terminal-position} geometry: the square positions of a semiprime sieve are the trivial factorization and Fermat's terminal position $2(b+j) = p+q$, which itself obeys the magnitude law $2bj_0 \le d^2$.

The parsimonious picture that survives is: within-$N$ small-$j$ clustering $=$ the linear magnitude law $+$ a positionally uniform divisibility enrichment $+$ a thin harmonic sliver.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The sieve polynomial}

Fermat's factorization method searches for a representation $N = x^2 - k^2$, from which $N = (x-k)(x+k)$ is immediate. Starting at $b = \lceil \sqrt N\,\rceil$ and sweeping upwards, it examines the values
\[
v(j) \;=\; (b+j)^2 - N, \qquad j = 0, 1, 2, \dots
\]
and halts when $v(j)$ is a perfect square. Congruence-of-squares methods --- the quadratic sieve and its relatives --- relax the halting condition: they collect positions where $v(j)$ is \textbf{$B$-smooth}, i.e.\ all prime factors are $< B$, and assemble a square by linear algebra over $\mathbb{F}_2$ on exponent vectors. Such a position is a \textbf{hit}.

\begin{definition}[Sieve polynomial and hits]
For integers $b,N$ put $v_{b,N}(j) = (b+j)^2 - N$. Given a smoothness bound $B$, position $j$ is a \emph{hit} if every prime factor of $|v_{b,N}(j)|$ is smaller than $B$. We write $v(j)$ when $b,N$ are clear, and $v(0) = b^2 - N$ for the \emph{base value}.
\end{definition}

\subsection{The empirical signal}

The motivating measurement, on a population of $128$ balanced semiprimes of bit length $96$ with hit positions recorded from $150{,}000$ sampled positions per modulus, is:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Overdispersion.} Mean $74.95$ hits per modulus, range $29$--$136$, far broader than Poisson; replicated four times, with $39$--$61\%$ of the between-$N$ variance unexplained by known rate factors.
  \item \textbf{Positional non-uniformity.} Rescaling each hit's position to $u \in [0,1]$ and pooling $9565$ hits from $127$ hit-carrying moduli, the empirical distribution of $u$ deviates from $U[0,1]$ with $D = 0.09519$ at overwhelming significance; a paired control of non-hits gives $D = 0.00693$, consistent with the null.
  \item \textbf{Decile profile.} $0.162,\ 0.123,\ 0.109,\ 0.097,\ 0.091,\ 0.091,\ 0.090,\ 0.084,\ 0.081,\ 0.072$: monotone declining, against a flat control.
  \item \textbf{Stratification.} Conditioning on eight cells of (bit-length of $|v|$) $\times$ (mantissa octant), the pooled discrepancy \emph{rises} to $D = 0.10423$; $7$ of $8$ cells are individually significant (median cell $p = 1.86\times 10^{-5}$); a within-cell permutation test gives $p < 0.0005$.
  \item \textbf{Null legs.} Lag-$1..10$ autocorrelation $\rho = +0.00283$, CI $[0.00112,0.00475]$, below the pre-registered $0.05$ threshold; edge fraction $0.2346$ versus baseline $0.20$, below the pre-registered $0.25$ threshold. Neither fires; controls null.
\end{itemize}

This paper asks what is arithmetically \emph{possible}: which mechanisms could produce a small-$j$ excess, how large an excess each can produce, and what the stratification protocol actually controls.

\section{The position--GCD law and the gcd carrier}

\begin{lemma}[Base expansion]\label{lem:expand}
For all $b,N,j \in \Z$, \quad $v(j) - v(0) = j\,(j + 2b)$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
$((b+j)^2 - N) - (b^2 - N) = 2bj + j^2 = j(j+2b)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Strict monotonicity]\label{lem:mono}
If $b \ge 0$ and $0 \le j_1 < j_2$ then $v(j_1) < v(j_2)$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
$0 \le b + j_1 < b + j_2$, so $(b+j_1)^2 < (b+j_2)^2$; subtract $N$.
\end{proof}

On the relevant range, position and magnitude are strictly increasing functions of each other; this returns with force in Section~\ref{sec:magnitude}.

\begin{theorem}[Position--GCD Law]\label{thm:gcdlaw}
For all $b,N,j \in \Z$, \quad $\gcd\bigl(j,\, v(j)\bigr) = \gcd\bigl(j,\, v(0)\bigr)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By Lemma~\ref{lem:expand}, $v(j) = v(0) + (j+2b)\,j$; adding a multiple of $j$ to the second argument does not change its gcd with $j$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Self-divisibility form]\label{cor:selfdvd}
$j \mid v(j) \iff j \mid v(0)$.
\end{corollary}

The arithmetic relation between a position and the value it carries is governed entirely by the fixed integer $v(0) = b^2 - N$, computable before the sieve starts.

\begin{theorem}[Free cofactor reduction]\label{thm:cofactor}
Let $B$ be a smoothness bound, $v$ a positive integer, and $g \mid v$ with $0 < g < B$. Then $v$ is $B$-smooth if and only if $v/g$ is $B$-smooth.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
($\Rightarrow$) A divisor of a $B$-smooth number is $B$-smooth. ($\Leftarrow$) Every positive integer $g < B$ is $B$-smooth, since all its prime factors are $\le g < B$; the $B$-smooth numbers are closed under multiplication, so $g\cdot(v/g) = v$ is $B$-smooth.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[The gcd carrier]\label{cor:carrier}
Put $g(j) = \gcd(j, v(0))$. If $0 < g(j) < B$ then position $j$ is a hit if and only if the reduced cofactor $|v(j)|/g(j)$ is $B$-smooth. In particular, if $0 < j < B$ and $j \mid v(0)$, then $|v(j)|$ is $B$-smooth as soon as $|v(j)/j|$ is.
\end{corollary}

This is a genuinely \emph{beyond-magnitude} enrichment: the effective quantity tested at position $j$ is smaller than $v(j)$ by a factor depending only on the arithmetic of $j$ against $v(0)$. Measured at matched magnitude, positions with nontrivial $g(j)$ hit about $21\%$ more often, with a monotone dose--response in $\log g$ (standardized effect $z = +24.2$).

\section{Local carriers are positionally uniform}

\begin{definition}[Local position property]
A property $P \subseteq \Z$ of positions is \emph{local of modulus $T$} if there is $Q \subseteq \Z/T\Z$ with $P(j) \iff Q(j \bmod T)$ for all $j$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Window equidistribution]\label{thm:window}
Let $P$ be local of modulus $T \ge 1$ with residue set $Q$. Then for every $a \in \Z$,
\[
\#\{\, 0 \le i < T : P(a+i) \,\} = \#Q,
\]
independently of $a$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The map $i \mapsto (a+i) \bmod T$ is a bijection from $\{0,\dots,T-1\}$ onto $\Z/T\Z$, carrying the positions satisfying $P$ exactly onto $Q$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Two residue classes per prime]\label{thm:twoclasses}
Let $p$ be prime. The set of residues $x \bmod p$ with $(b+x)^2 \equiv N \pmod p$ has cardinality at most $2$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
If $r$ is a solution and $x$ is any solution, $(b+x)^2 - (b+r)^2 = (x-r)(x+r+2b) \equiv 0 \pmod p$, so $x \equiv r$ or $x \equiv -r-2b$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Exact positional uniformity of prime divisibility]
For every prime $p$, each window of $p$ consecutive positions contains the same number of positions $j$ with $p \mid v(j)$, and that number is at most $2$. No single small prime can generate a small-$j$ excess.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Prime powers give two classes]\label{thm:ppow}
Let $p$ be an odd prime with $p \nmid N$ and $k \ge 1$. If $p^k \mid v(x)$ and $p^k \mid v(y)$ then $p^k \mid (x-y)$ or $p^k \mid (x+y+2b)$. Equivalently, fixing one solution $r$, every solution satisfies $x \equiv r$ or $x \equiv -r-2b \pmod{p^k}$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
From $v(x) - v(y) = (x-y)(x+y+2b)$ we get $p^k \mid (x-y)(x+y+2b)$. Since $p \mid v(x)$ and $p \nmid N$, we have $p \nmid (b+x)$: otherwise $p \mid (b+x)^2 - v(x) = N$. As $p$ is odd, $p \nmid 2$, so $p$ cannot divide both factors, whose sum is $2(b+x)$. Hence all of $p^k$ divides whichever factor $p$ divides.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Positional uniformity of the gcd carrier]\label{thm:gcduniform}
Suppose $v(0) \ne 0$. The set of positions with $\gcd(j, v(0)) > 1$ is local of modulus $|v(0)|$; consequently every window of $|v(0)|$ consecutive positions contains the same number of such positions.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$\gcd(j,v(0)) = \gcd(j \bmod v(0),\, v(0))$ depends only on $j \bmod v(0)$; apply Theorem~\ref{thm:window}.
\end{proof}

Combining Corollary~\ref{cor:carrier} with Theorem~\ref{thm:gcduniform}: the gcd carrier is a real, magnitude-free smoothness enrichment which is exactly uniform in position, and therefore cannot be the source of an observed small-$j$ excess.

\subsection{A discrepancy bound as a falsification instrument}

Write $\cnt_P(a,L) = \#\{0 \le i < L : P(a+i)\}$.

\begin{lemma}[Additivity]\label{lem:add}
$\cnt_P(a, L_1+L_2) = \cnt_P(a,L_1) + \cnt_P(a+L_1, L_2)$, and $\cnt_P(a,L) \le L$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{lemma}[Full periods]\label{lem:periods}
If $P$ is local of modulus $T$ with residue set $Q$, then $\cnt_P(a, Tm) = m\,\#Q$ for every $a$ and $m \ge 0$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Induction on $m$ using Lemma~\ref{lem:add} and Theorem~\ref{thm:window}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Window bounds]\label{thm:windowbounds}
Let $P$ be local of modulus $T$ with residue set $Q$, and $L = Tm + r$ with $0 \le r < T$. Then for every $a$,
\[
m\,\#Q \;\le\; \cnt_P(a,L) \;\le\; m\,\#Q + T .
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Split $L$ into $m$ full periods and a remainder of length $r < T$; the remainder contributes between $0$ and $r \le T$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Local block balance]\label{thm:balance}
Let $P$ be local of modulus $T$. Then for any two windows of the same length $L$,
$\bigl|\cnt_P(a,L) - \cnt_P(a',L)\bigr| \le T$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Both counts lie in the interval $[m\#Q,\ m\#Q+T]$ of Theorem~\ref{thm:windowbounds}.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Modulus lower bound]\label{cor:modbound}
If two equally long blocks of sieve positions differ by $E$ hits, then every local explanation of the excess must have modulus $T \ge E$: all carriers of modulus $T < E$ are ruled out by that single observation.
\end{corollary}

\section{The unique declining carrier: exact $1/j$ density}

\begin{theorem}[One multiple per window]\label{thm:onemult}
For $d \ge 1$ and any $a \in \Z$, exactly one of $a, a+1, \dots, a+d-1$ is divisible by $d$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Divisibility by $d$ is local of modulus $d$ with residue set $\{0\}$; apply Theorem~\ref{thm:window}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Exact density $1/d$]\label{thm:density}
For $d \ge 1$, $t \ge 0$ and any $a$, exactly $t$ of the $dt$ consecutive integers $a,\dots,a+dt-1$ are divisible by $d$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Induction on $t$: split $[a, a+d(t+1))$ into $[a, a+dt)$ and the length-$d$ window starting at $a+dt$, and apply Theorem~\ref{thm:onemult}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Harmonic block decline]\label{thm:harmonic}
For every integer $K \ge 1$,
\[
\sum_{j=K+1}^{2K} \frac{1}{j} \;<\; \sum_{j=1}^{K} \frac{1}{j}.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The left sum has exactly $K$ terms, each at most $1/(K+1)$, hence is at most $K/(K+1) < 1$. The right sum contains the term $j=1$ and all terms are nonnegative, hence is at least $1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Small-$j$ excess of the self-divisibility carrier]\label{thm:excess}
Let $K \ge 1$ and let $M \ge 1$ be divisible by every $j \in [1,2K]$. For any $a \in \Z$,
\[
\sum_{j=K+1}^{2K} \#\{\, 0 \le i < M : j \mid a+i \,\}
\;<\;
\sum_{j=1}^{K} \#\{\, 0 \le i < M : j \mid a+i \,\}.
\]
Equivalently, averaged over base values, the expected number of positions $j \in [1,K]$ with $j \mid v(j)$ strictly exceeds the expected number in the next block $(K, 2K]$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By Theorem~\ref{thm:density}, for each $j \le 2K$ the inner count equals $M/j$ exactly. Both sides are $M$ times the corresponding harmonic sum; apply Theorem~\ref{thm:harmonic} and multiply by $M>0$.
\end{proof}

Thus the shape observed in the data --- monotone decline across position blocks --- has an exact, magnitude-free arithmetic ancestor. Two caveats: its mass is a harmonic tail, and by Theorem~\ref{thm:balance} its per-window discrepancy is bounded by its modulus.

\section{The magnitude channel and the collapse of size-stratification}\label{sec:magnitude}

Assume $b = \lceil\sqrt N\,\rceil$, made precise as $(b-1)^2 \le N \le b^2$.

\begin{theorem}[Magnitude sandwich]\label{thm:sandwich}
For $j \ge 0$ and $(b-1)^2 \le N \le b^2$,
\[
2bj \;\le\; v(j) \;\le\; 2bj + j^2 + 2b .
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Write $v(j) = 2bj + j^2 + (b^2 - N)$. Since $N \le b^2$, $b^2 - N \ge 0$ and $v(j) \ge 2bj + j^2 \ge 2bj$. Since $(b-1)^2 \le N$, $b^2 - N \le 2b-1 \le 2b$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Position bound from a value bound]
If $j \ge 0$ and $v(j) \le X$ then $2bj \le X$, i.e.\ $j \le X/(2b)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Initial-block localization]
For $b \ge 1$, the number of positions $j \ge 1$ with $v(j) \le X$ is at most $\lfloor X/(2b)\rfloor$. Conversely, for $2 \le j \le b$ one has $v(j) \le 4bj$. Hence the sub-$X$ part of the sieve is an initial block of positions of length between $X/(4b)$ and $X/(2b)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The first claim is the previous theorem plus counting. For the second, $v(j) = 2bj + j^2 + (b^2-N) \le 2bj + jb + 2b \le 4bj$ when $2 \le j \le b$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Size classes are position intervals]\label{thm:ordconn}
Let $b \ge 0$ and $f$ be any monotone function of the value. If $f(v(j_1)) = f(v(j_2)) = c$ and $0 \le j_1 \le j \le j_2$, then $f(v(j)) = c$: every magnitude class is an order-connected set of positions.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By Lemma~\ref{lem:mono}, $v$ is increasing on $j \ge 0$; hence $f \circ v$ is monotone and $c \le f(v(j)) \le c$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Cell collapse]\label{thm:cell}
Let $b \ge 1$, $(b-1)^2 \le N$, and suppose $v(j_2) \le 2\,v(j_1)$ (a one-bit magnitude cell). Then
\[
b\,j_2 \;\le\; 2b\,j_1 + 2b + j_1^2, \qquad\text{i.e.}\qquad j_2 \le 2j_1 + 2 + \frac{j_1^2}{b}.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Expand $(b+j_2)^2 - N \le 2\bigl((b+j_1)^2 - N\bigr)$ and use $N \ge (b-1)^2$; the resulting quadratic inequality reduces to the stated linear bound after discarding nonnegative squares.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Sharp small-position form]
If in addition $j_1^2 \le b$, then $j_2 \le 2j_1 + 3$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{remark}
Within a single modulus, conditioning on the bit-length (or bit-length times mantissa octant) of $|v|$ confines the positions in a cell to a factor-two window (Theorem~\ref{thm:cell}), and each cell is an interval of positions (Theorem~\ref{thm:ordconn}). There is nothing within a cell to decorrelate. Hence the observed strengthening of the discrepancy under stratification ($0.09519 \to 0.10423$) cannot be read as magnitude-free within-$N$ geometry without a control that breaks Theorem~\ref{thm:ordconn} --- for instance a cross-modulus comparison at matched $|v|$ but widely differing $j$. Performed at matched $|v|$ and $256$-fold different positions, hit rates agree to within $4\%$ (weighted ratio $R = 0.983$): no positional excess detected.
\end{remark}

\section{Non-locality of the smooth locus}

Consider the \emph{degenerate sieve} $b = 1$, $N = 0$, with values $v(j) = (j+1)^2$ and smoothness bound $B = 3$.

\begin{lemma}[$3$-smooth numbers]
A positive integer $n$ is $3$-smooth if and only if $n = 2^k$ for some $k \ge 0$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{lemma}[Hit positions of the degenerate sieve]
For $i \ge 0$, position $i$ is a hit if and only if $i+1$ is a power of two.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
The value is $(i+1)^2$, a power of two iff $i+1$ is.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Unbounded block imbalance]\label{thm:imbalance}
For every $n \ge 0$, the block $[0,2^n)$ contains at least $n+1$ hits while the block $[2^n, 2^{n+1})$ of the same length contains at most one.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The positions $2^k - 1$, $k = 0,\dots,n$, are distinct and lie in $[0,2^n)$. In $[2^n, 2^{n+1})$, a hit at $i$ requires $i+1 = 2^k$ with $2^n < 2^k \le 2^{n+1}$, forcing $k = n+1$ and $i = 2^{n+1}-1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The smooth locus is not local]\label{thm:nonlocal}
For every $T$ there are two equally long blocks of positions whose hit counts differ by more than $T$. Consequently no modulus $T$ and no set of residues $Q \subseteq \Z/T\Z$ describes the positions carrying smooth values.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Take $n = T+1$ in Theorem~\ref{thm:imbalance}: the imbalance is at least $T+1$. If the locus were local of modulus $T$, Theorem~\ref{thm:balance} would bound the imbalance by $T$.
\end{proof}

Local carriers have positional discrepancy at most their modulus; the smooth locus has unbounded positional discrepancy. The only mechanism with unbounded positional reach is the magnitude law of Theorem~\ref{thm:sandwich}.

\section{Exact geometry of the terminal Fermat position}

\begin{theorem}[Square positions are factorizations]\label{thm:sq}
For all $b,N,j,k$: \quad $v(j) = k^2 \iff N = (b+j-k)(b+j+k)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Terminal position]
If $N = s^2 - d^2$ then $v(s-b) = d^2$. In particular, for $N = pq$ with $p+q = 2s$, $q-p = 2d$, the position $j_0 = s - b$ carries the perfect square $d^2$: the terminal Fermat position.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[The terminal position obeys the magnitude law]
If $b \le s$ and $s^2 - d^2 \le b^2$, then $2b(s-b) \le d^2$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$2b(s-b) \le (s-b)(s+b) = s^2 - b^2 \le d^2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Uniqueness of square positions for semiprimes]
Let $p \le q$ be primes, $N = pq$. If $v(j) = k^2$ with $k \ge 0$ and $b+j-k > 1$, then $2(b+j) = p+q$. Every other square position is the trivial factorization $b+j-k = 1$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By Theorem~\ref{thm:sq}, $(b+j-k)(b+j+k) = pq$ with $1 < b+j-k \le b+j+k$. The ordered factorizations of a semiprime are $(1,pq)$ and $(p,q)$; excluding the first leaves $b+j-k = p$, $b+j+k = q$, and adding gives $2(b+j) = p+q$.
\end{proof}

\section{Synthesis}

\begin{enumerate}
  \item A beyond-magnitude carrier exists: $g(j) = \gcd(j,v(0))$ can be divided out for free when $g(j) < B$ (Theorems~\ref{thm:gcdlaw}, \ref{thm:cofactor}), empirically worth about $21\%$ in hit rate at matched magnitude.
  \item But it is positionally flat (Theorems~\ref{thm:window}, \ref{thm:gcduniform}), as is divisibility by any prime or prime power (Theorems~\ref{thm:twoclasses}, \ref{thm:ppow}).
  \item Only the full-divisor sub-carrier declines, with exact density $1/j$ and a proved harmonic block decline (Theorems~\ref{thm:density}--\ref{thm:excess}); its mass is small.
  \item Every local mechanism is capped: an excess of $E$ hits forces modulus $T \ge E$ (Corollary~\ref{cor:modbound}).
  \item Magnitude cannot be conditioned away within one modulus (Theorems~\ref{thm:ordconn}, \ref{thm:cell}).
  \item The smooth locus is not local (Theorem~\ref{thm:nonlocal}).
  \item The one exactly known position is a magnitude phenomenon.
  \item Cross-scale evidence is null ($R = 0.983$ at $256$-fold position separation).
\end{enumerate}

\noindent\textbf{The parsimonious picture.} Within-$N$ small-$j$ clustering $=$ the magnitude law $2bj \le v(j) \le 2bj + j^2 + 2b$, plus a positionally uniform divisibility enrichment governed by $v(0)$, plus a thin harmonic sliver from full self-divisibility. The measured signal is not explained away --- it is made expensive: any surviving explanation must be non-local, or have modulus at least the observed excess, or be a cross-modulus effect.

\section{Algorithms and applications}

\paragraph{Free-cofactor sieving.} Compute $v(0) = b^2 - N$ once. At each position $j < B$ compute $g = \gcd(j, v(0))$ in $O(\log j)$ and test $|v(j)|/g$ rather than $|v(j)|$: a factor-$g$ reduction at negligible cost, with the number of discounted positions in a block predicted exactly by Theorem~\ref{thm:gcduniform}.

\paragraph{Modulus lower bound as a reporting standard.} Corollary~\ref{cor:modbound} converts a measured block excess $E$ into a lower bound on the modulus of any local explanation. Experiments reporting positional structure should report $E$ alongside the $p$-value: $E$ excludes mechanisms, $p$-values exclude only chance.

\paragraph{Stratification design.} Within a single modulus, do not stratify by $|v|$ --- it is a no-op (Theorems~\ref{thm:ordconn}, \ref{thm:cell}). Valid designs compare across moduli at matched $|v|$ and dissimilar $j$, or match $j$ and vary $|v|$ by changing $b$.

\paragraph{Calibration by the terminal position.} The terminal Fermat position $j_0 = s-b$ with $2bj_0 \le d^2$ is an exactly known hit; any positional statistic should assign it the score the magnitude law predicts, not more.

\section{Future directions}

\paragraph{D1. Harmonic law for the full-divisor carrier.} Since $j \mid v(j) \iff j \mid v(0)$, the only declining carrier is a divisor-counting problem for the single integer $v(0)$, so its profile should be $d(v(0))$-shaped rather than merely $1/j$-shaped. Conjecture: for a random base value the expected number of self-divisor positions in $[1,J]$ is $\log J + 2\gamma - 1 + o(1)$; the mass of the block $(K,2K]$ converges to $\log 2$, so the small-$j$ excess of $[1,K]$ over $(K,2K]$ widens like $\log K + \gamma - \log 2$.

\paragraph{D2. Modulus lower bound as a falsification instrument.} Make Corollary~\ref{cor:modbound} a standard reporting requirement; combined with Theorem~\ref{thm:nonlocal} it gives a two-sided classification of admissible mechanisms.

\paragraph{D3. Functional form of the small-$j$ profile.} Fit the observed decile profile against the magnitude-only prediction from the Dickman--de Bruijn heuristic and quantify the residual, which the theory says should be flat in $j$ up to a harmonic tail.

\paragraph{D4. Positional $\leftrightarrow$ rate link.} Does $j$-local clustering predict which moduli are hit-rich, connecting within-$N$ geometry to the $39$--$61\%$ unexplained between-$N$ overdispersion?

\paragraph{D5. Multiple-polynomial designs.} Varying $b$ at fixed $N$ changes $v(0)$ while holding magnitude fixed: the cleanest available experimental separation of the two channels.

\section{Conclusion}

The interaction between a sieve position and its own value is governed by a single integer through the position--gcd law; this yields a genuine magnitude-free smoothness enrichment which is nevertheless exactly uniform in position; the unique declining sub-carrier is full self-divisibility, with density $1/j$ and a proved harmonic block decline; every congruence-type mechanism is capped by its modulus; the smooth locus itself admits no congruence description; and the magnitude channel ties position to size so tightly that size-stratification within a modulus is vacuous. These results replace a vague dichotomy --- magnitude or structure? --- with a precise inventory of what each mechanism can and cannot produce.

